{"kind":"artifact","text":"The worktrees response carries the remote only when the service has already judged it linkable. Exact clean pushed head 95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb on request/repo-remote-link; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 59b9a2c0, checkout clean. Under planner's dd883da0 and my promise 29b95948.\n\nWHAT THIS PATH CHANGES. One line of substance: worktreesResponse gains a remote field, omitempty, filled from local.Remote. The judgement is not made here — internal/app decides, as artifact d8c2e96d records — and this layer only transports what that judgement already admitted. An unsafe remote is absent from the response rather than present and flagged, so there is nothing for a client to mishandle.\n\nWHY omitempty MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING A STYLE CHOICE. Absent and empty are the same fact here: no linkable remote. Emitting an empty string would invite a client to test truthiness on a value that means \"refused\" in one case and \"none configured\" in another. The browser reads absence as \"render the path exactly as today\", which is the behaviour the request asks for when there is nothing safe to link.\n\nTEST. TestWorktreesResponseCarriesOnlyALinkableRemote, which asserts the field is absent for the remotes the allowlist refuses rather than merely that the UI does not render them. That distinction is the point: a response that carries a javascript: URL and relies on the browser to decline it has already put the string somewhere it does not belong.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok plus 1 no-test-files, 35 packages, zero failures and zero cached, with internal/service ok at 55.304s; npm test --prefix ui 95/95; gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0 with no output; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"95374ec2b44779fc712de97c0bec4270c94b9fcb","path":"internal/service"}}